      {
        "id": "u1",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "Definition of stoichiometry",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "statement"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s1"
        ],
        "rationale": "Introduces the topic, demystifies the term, and provides the core functional definition of stoichiometry.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "everyday",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "passage_id": "p3",
              "quote": "cooking with chemistry"
            }
          ]
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p1",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "social"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Welcome to chemistry class! ",
            "start": 0,
            "end": 28
          },
          {
            "id": "p2",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "First, let’s address the elephant in the room: **“Stoichiometry”** (pronounced *stoy-kee-AHM-uh-tree*) sounds like an intimidating, ancient spell. But I promise you, it isn't. ",
            "start": 30,
            "end": 206
          },
          {
            "id": "p3",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "At its core, **stoichiometry is simply cooking with chemistry.** It’s the math we use to figure out how much stuff we need to start with, or how much stuff we will end up with, during a chemical reaction.",
            "start": 208,
            "end": 412
          },
          {
            "id": "p4",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Let’s break it down step-by-step.",
            "start": 414,
            "end": 447
          },
          {
            "id": "p5",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 449,
            "end": 452
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The definition correctly describes stoichiometry as the branch of chemistry dealing with quantitative relationships between reactants and products in chemical reactions.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u2",
        "kind": "ANALOGY",
        "label": "Kitchen recipe analogy for balanced chemical equations",
        "attributes": {},
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s2"
        ],
        "rationale": "Uses a grilled cheese sandwich recipe to explain how stoichiometric coefficients in balanced chemical equations dictate reactant and product ratios.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "everyday",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "passage_id": "p7",
              "quote": "Imagine you are making grilled cheese sandwiches."
            }
          ]
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p6",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Step 1: The Kitchen Analogy (The Recipe)",
            "start": 454,
            "end": 498
          },
          {
            "id": "p7",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Imagine you are making grilled cheese sandwiches. Here is your recipe:",
            "start": 500,
            "end": 570
          },
          {
            "id": "p8",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$\\text{2 slices of Bread} + \\text{1 slice of Cheese} \\rightarrow \\text{1 Grilled Cheese}$$",
            "start": 572,
            "end": 663
          },
          {
            "id": "p9",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* If you have **8 slices of bread** (and plenty of cheese), how many sandwiches can you make? **4 sandwiches.**",
            "start": 665,
            "end": 776
          },
          {
            "id": "p10",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* If you want to make **10 sandwiches**, how many slices of bread do you need? **20 slices.**",
            "start": 777,
            "end": 870
          },
          {
            "id": "p11",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Congratulations—you just did stoichiometry! ",
            "start": 872,
            "end": 916
          },
          {
            "id": "p12",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "In chemistry, our \"recipe\" is a **balanced chemical equation**. ",
            "start": 918,
            "end": 982
          },
          {
            "id": "p13",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Look at how water is made:",
            "start": 983,
            "end": 1009
          },
          {
            "id": "p14",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$2\\text{H}_2 + \\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$",
            "start": 1011,
            "end": 1071
          },
          {
            "id": "p15",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "This tells us: **2 molecules of hydrogen** react with **1 molecule of oxygen** to make **2 molecules of water**. The numbers in front (the coefficients) are just the recipe's ingredient ratios.",
            "start": 1073,
            "end": 1266
          },
          {
            "id": "p16",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 1268,
            "end": 1271
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The analogy accurately illustrates how stoichiometric coefficients function as ingredient ratios, mapped directly onto the formation reaction of water.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u3",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "The necessity of the mole due to atomic mass differences",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "explanation"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s3"
        ],
        "rationale": "Explains why mass ratios cannot directly replace particle ratios in stoichiometry due to differences in atomic mass among elements.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "everyday",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "passage_id": "p18",
              "quote": "In the kitchen, you can physically count 2 slices of bread."
            }
          ]
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p17",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Step 2: The Problem (We Can't Count Atoms)",
            "start": 1273,
            "end": 1319
          },
          {
            "id": "p18",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "In the kitchen, you can physically count 2 slices of bread. But in a chemistry lab, atoms are way too tiny to see or count. You can't say, *\"Let me grab 200 hydrogen atoms.\"* ",
            "start": 1321,
            "end": 1496
          },
          {
            "id": "p19",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Instead, chemists **weigh** things on a scale in grams. ",
            "start": 1498,
            "end": 1554
          },
          {
            "id": "p20",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "However, you can't just say *\"2 grams of hydrogen + 1 gram of oxygen = 2 grams of water.\"* Why? Because an oxygen atom is **16 times heavier** than a hydrogen atom! 100 grams of hydrogen has way more atoms in it than 100 grams of oxygen.",
            "start": 1556,
            "end": 1793
          },
          {
            "id": "p21",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "To fix this, chemists invented a unit called **The Mole**.",
            "start": 1795,
            "end": 1853
          },
          {
            "id": "p22",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 1855,
            "end": 1858
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The text accurately explains that atoms cannot be counted directly and that equal masses of different elements do not contain equal numbers of atoms, necessitating a bridging counting unit.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u4",
        "kind": "CONCEPT",
        "label": "The mole concept and molar mass",
        "attributes": {
          "depth": "explanation"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s4"
        ],
        "rationale": "Defines the mole as Avogadro's number using the dozen analogy and explains how the periodic table provides the molar mass to convert grams to moles.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "everyday",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "passage_id": "p25",
              "quote": "A dozen eggs = 12 eggs."
            }
          ]
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p23",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Step 3: The Secret Tool — The \"Mole\"",
            "start": 1860,
            "end": 1900
          },
          {
            "id": "p24",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Think of the word **\"dozen.\"** When I say \"a dozen,\" you think of the number **12**. ",
            "start": 1902,
            "end": 1987
          },
          {
            "id": "p25",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* A dozen eggs = 12 eggs. ",
            "start": 1988,
            "end": 2014
          },
          {
            "id": "p26",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* A dozen cars = 12 cars. ",
            "start": 2015,
            "end": 2041
          },
          {
            "id": "p27",
            "category": "ANALOGY",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "The cars weigh a lot more than the eggs, but there are still 12 of each.",
            "start": 2042,
            "end": 2114
          },
          {
            "id": "p28",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "**A \"mole\" is just a chemist's dozen.** It’s just a specific number: **$6.022 \\times 10^{23}$** (a huge number, because atoms are tiny). ",
            "start": 2116,
            "end": 2253
          },
          {
            "id": "p29",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "statement"
            },
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "The best part? You can convert grams into moles using the **Periodic Table**:",
            "start": 2255,
            "end": 2332
          },
          {
            "id": "p30",
            "category": "CONCEPT",
            "attributes": {
              "depth": "explanation"
            },
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* Find Oxygen on the table: its mass is about **16 grams**. That means $1 \\text{ mole of Oxygen atoms} = 16 \\text{ grams}$.",
            "start": 2333,
            "end": 2456
          },
          {
            "id": "p31",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 2458,
            "end": 2461
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The mole is accurately defined using Avogadro's constant (6.022 x 10^23) and connected correctly to atomic/molar mass from the periodic table.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u5",
        "kind": "PROCEDURE",
        "label": "Three-step stoichiometry problem-solving map",
        "attributes": {},
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s5"
        ],
        "rationale": "Presents a reusable general procedure for converting from grams of a reactant to grams of a product via mole conversions and the mole ratio.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "none",
          "evidence": []
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p32",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Step 4: The Master Plan (The Stoichiometry Map)",
            "start": 2463,
            "end": 2514
          },
          {
            "id": "p33",
            "category": "PROCEDURE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "Whenever you get a stoichiometry problem, it almost always follows this **3-step journey**:",
            "start": 2516,
            "end": 2607
          },
          {
            "id": "p34",
            "category": "PROCEDURE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$\\text{Grams of A} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Step 1}} \\text{Moles of A} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Step 2}} \\text{Moles of B} \\xrightarrow{\\text{Step 3}} \\text{Grams of B}$$",
            "start": 2609,
            "end": 2768
          },
          {
            "id": "p35",
            "category": "PROCEDURE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "1. **Grams to Moles:** Weigh your starting chemical and convert it to moles (using the Periodic Table).",
            "start": 2770,
            "end": 2873
          },
          {
            "id": "p36",
            "category": "PROCEDURE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "2. **Mole to Mole (The Recipe Bridge):** Use the numbers from your balanced equation to switch from Chemical A to Chemical B.",
            "start": 2874,
            "end": 2999
          },
          {
            "id": "p37",
            "category": "PROCEDURE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "3. **Moles to Grams:** Convert your new moles back into grams so you know what it weighs (using the Periodic Table again).",
            "start": 3000,
            "end": 3122
          },
          {
            "id": "p38",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "separator"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "---",
            "start": 3124,
            "end": 3127
          }
        ],
        "accuracy": {
          "verdict": "accurate",
          "reason": "The 3-step sequence (mass A -> moles A -> moles B -> mass B) accurately outlines the standard algorithmic pathway for mass-mass stoichiometry calculations.",
          "errors": []
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "u6",
        "kind": "EXAMPLE",
        "label": "Worked mass-to-mass calculation of water production from oxygen",
        "attributes": {
          "context": "abstract_or_hypothetical",
          "treatment": "worked"
        },
        "subtopic_ids": [
          "s5",
          "s6"
        ],
        "rationale": "Walks through a complete calculation determining the mass of water formed from 32 grams of oxygen gas.",
        "review_flags": [],
        "contextualization": {
          "value": "none",
          "evidence": []
        },
        "passages": [
          {
            "id": "p39",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "### Let's Do a Real Example Together!",
            "start": 3129,
            "end": 3166
          },
          {
            "id": "p40",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "**Question:** How many grams of water ($\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$) can you make from **32 grams of Oxygen gas ($\\text{O}_2$)**?",
            "start": 3168,
            "end": 3289
          },
          {
            "id": "p41",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "**The Recipe:** ",
            "start": 3291,
            "end": 3307
          },
          {
            "id": "p42",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$2\\text{H}_2 + 1\\text{O}_2 \\rightarrow 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$$",
            "start": 3308,
            "end": 3369
          },
          {
            "id": "p43",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "#### **Step 1: Convert Grams of $\\text{O}_2$ to Moles of $\\text{O}_2$**",
            "start": 3371,
            "end": 3442
          },
          {
            "id": "p44",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* One Oxygen atom is 16g. Since $\\text{O}_2$ has two, the molar mass is $16 \\times 2 = 32\\text{ g/mol}$.",
            "start": 3443,
            "end": 3547
          },
          {
            "id": "p45",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* You have 32 grams:",
            "start": 3548,
            "end": 3568
          },
          {
            "id": "p46",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$\\frac{32\\text{ g}}{32\\text{ g/mol}} = \\mathbf{1\\text{ mole of } O_2}$$",
            "start": 3569,
            "end": 3641
          },
          {
            "id": "p47",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "#### **Step 2: Use the Recipe (Mole Ratio)**",
            "start": 3643,
            "end": 3687
          },
          {
            "id": "p48",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* Look at the coefficients in the reaction:",
            "start": 3688,
            "end": 3731
          },
          {
            "id": "p49",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "  * For every **$1\\text{ O}_2$**, you produce **$2\\text{ H}_2\\text{O}$**.",
            "start": 3732,
            "end": 3805
          },
          {
            "id": "p50",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "list",
              "prose"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "* So, if you have $1\\text{ mole of } \\text{O}_2$, you will make:",
            "start": 3806,
            "end": 3870
          },
          {
            "id": "p51",
            "category": "EXAMPLE",
            "attributes": {},
            "formats": [
              "equation"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "$$1 \\times 2 = \\mathbf{2\\text{ moles of } H_2O}$$",
            "start": 3871,
            "end": 3920
          },
          {
            "id": "p52",
            "category": "ORGANIZATION",
            "attributes": {
              "subtype": "structural"
            },
            "formats": [
              "heading"
            ],
            "review_flags": [],
            "text": "#### **Step 3: Convert Moles of $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$ to Grams**",
            "start": 3922,
            "end": 3985
          },
          {
